## The same paragraph, four ways {#p-four}

The document's `translate-to = "zh-cn"` is the default, so a bare embed lands in
Chinese:

=== embed {src=ch07-source.geml#s-top-before-s-cmd}
===

An embed may override the axis. Japanese:

=== embed {src=ch07-source.geml#s-top-before-s-cmd translate-to=ja}
===

French:

=== embed {src=ch07-source.geml#s-top-before-s-cmd translate-to=fr}
===

And `translate-to=none` holds one back, which is how a quotation stays a
quotation:

=== embed {src=ch07-source.geml#s-top-before-s-cmd translate-to=none}
===

Four renderings, one paragraph, zero copies. Edit `ch07-source.geml` and all
four move together.

## What is never translated {#p-never}

The parser decides *what may be translated*: heading and paragraph text, list
items, table cells, a `caption=`. It never touches an id, a link target, or the
body of a `code`, `math` or `data` block — so a command survives the crossing
whatever the axis says:

=== embed {src=ch07-source.geml#s-cmd}
===

Table cells, by contrast, are prose an author wrote, so they do move — while the
terms pinned in [[#p-terms]] stay put:

=== embed {src=ch07-source.geml#s-artifacts}
===

## Who does the translating is not the format's business {#p-who}

The document declares the axis; it never names an engine. A CLI can hand the
renderer an LLM. This page hands it **Chrome's built-in on-device Translator**,
so the text is translated without leaving the machine. The VS Code extension
hands it the editor's language model. Same document, three backends, no change.

=== note {.warning #p-degrade}
Not in Chrome? The projection falls back to the source text and says which
browser it found and why there is no translator — a projection never fails
silently, and it never ships your document somewhere to get translated. The
first Chinese block above may also ask for a one-time model download; Chrome
only permits that under a click.
===
